65,"We implemented an explicit map- ping logic between the synthesized housing stock and 32 standardized UK building archetypes ([3]), with the full parameters detailed in Table 1."
68,"The household-level theoretical thermal energy re- quirementT h(kWh/year) is computed via a 5- dimensional Gaussian Process (GP) emulator trained onN= 9,600 EnergyPlus simulations."
69,"The simula- tion design is generated by Latin Hypercube Sampling(LHS) over five physical input dimensions for each of the 32 national archetypes: floor areaS h, external wall U-valueU w,h, air change raten ACH,h , window-to-wall ratioψ h, and localized Heating Degree Days (HDD)."
70,"The LHS design spans±30% of each age-band and form-band nominal value, ensuring full coverage of the within-archetype population variance captured during Phase 1 synthesis."
72,"First, to eliminate geographic orientation bias, each archetype is simulated across four cardinal orientations (North, East, South, West) and the resulting thermal demand is averaged in accordance with CIBSE TM54 guidelines."
73,"Second, to prevent the overestimation of 4 metabolic heat gains in large properties, the number of occupants is dynamically assigned using the asymp- totic occupancy curve defined in SAP 2012 Section 4.2."
74,"Finally, all dwellings are constrained to the standard- ized SAP 10.2 heating schedule (9 hours on weekdays, 16 hours on weekends at 21◦C)."
75,"By enforcing these rigid physical and operational parameters within En- ergyPlus v25.2, we decouple the true structural vari- ance from local behavioral noise."
77,"The GP emulator uses a Mat´ ern (ν= 2.5) kernel and a White noise term to absorb EnergyPlus roundtrip discretisation noise: Th=fGP␀ Sh, Uw,h, nACH,h , ψh ±σGP h (1) whereσGP his the GP predictive standard deviation."
80,"The emula- tor achieves a held-out predictiveR2>0.99 on 50 re- served EnergyPlus runs per archetype family, confirm- ing near-exact interpolation of the simulated response surface."
82,3.3 Bayesian Hierarchical Specification The core computational model characterizes physi- cal energy signals from socioeconomic noise.
83,"We employ a hierarchical model with an Intrinsic Con- ditional Auto-Regressive (ICAR) prior to capture spatial autocorrelation, building upon the founda- tional Bayesian calibration framework established by Kennedy and O’Hagan [12] and its subsequent refine- ments for macro-to-micro urban energy calibration [2]."
85,"To handle the structural dis- crepancy between the deterministic physics baseline and empirical observations, we utilize a model discrep- ancy function inspired by the multi-level frameworks of [16], ensuring that calibration parameters remain iden- tifiable even in the presence of unmodeled behavioral noise."
86,"We model log-consumption as a function of the physical baseline, deprivation, and localized random effects: ym∼Normal(µ m, σ2 y) µm= log(T m) +β th+βincZinc,m +θm+ϕmWhereT mrepresents the intrinsic structural require- ment derived from building physics, aggregated as the arithmetic mean of synthetic household requirements Tm=1 HmPHm h=1Tm,h."
91,"3.4 Bayesian Priors and Mathematical Jus- tification To ensure robust parameter estimation and prevent overfitting in a highly parameterized spatial model, we employ weakly informative priors tailored to the ex- pected scale of the effects."
92,"While this multi-level for- mulation is highly advantageous for predictive decou- pling and characterizing structural signals, the recov- ered group-level coefficients represent associative spa- tial elasticity rather than isolated causal effects [7]."
93,1.
95,"We apply a strongly informative prior derived from longitudinal studies of the UK ‘perfor- mance gap’ which indicate that SAP-based theoretical models consistently over-predict actual demand by ap- proximately 30% [26, 5]:β th∼Normal(−0.3,0.1) 2."
97,"We use a weakly informative Normal prior: βinc∼Normal(0,0.5) 3."
98,"Spatial Autocorrelation (ϕ mandθ m): We utilize a BYM2 (Besag-York-Mollie) specification ([20]), where the random effect is decomposed into a spatially structured component (ϕ m) and an un- structured component (θ m)."
100,"We specify the standard BYM2 hyperpriors for the spatial mixing parameter ρ∼Beta(0.5,0.5) and the total random effect stan- dard deviationσ total∼HalfNormal(1.0), ensuring an uninformative prior on the spatial scale while regular- izing the total neighborhood variance."
101,"To ensure ab- 5 solute model identifiability and prevent Hodges-Reich confounding (where the spatial intercept absorbs the global intercept), we explicitly enforce a strict sum- to-zero constraint across all nodes for the spatial field (Pϕm= 0) using a PyMC Potential."
102,"ϕ∼Normal(0,Σ) (2) WhereQ= Σ−1is the precision matrix proportional to the graph LaplacianL=D−W, withDthe diagonal degree matrix andWthe adjacency matrix."
103,3.5 Hardware and Computational Opti- mization Executing this model efficiently at the national scale required a fundamental departure from dense matrix operations.
