194,"5.5 Micro-scale Comparative Case Studies: Hampstead, Birmingham, and Oldham To demonstrate the decoupling power of the frame- work, we examine three divergent MSOAs:Hamp- stead Town,Birmingham 044(Ladywood), and Oldham 014."
195,"InHampstead Town (Camden 004), the theo- retical baselineT his estimated at 14,800 kWh/year."
197,"However, the observed mean gas consumptiony mis 39,220 kWh/year, yield- ing an empirical consumption ratio of 2.65."
199,"InBirmingham 044, a transitionary urban neigh- borhood, the theoretical baseline is 16,400 kWh/year."
201,The recovered ratio of 0.88 suggests a persistent 12% under-heating gap.
203,"Conversely, inOldham 014, a highly deprived neighborhood, the theoretical baseline is 20,450kWh/year—driven by a stock of solid-wall pre-1919 terraced housing."
204,"Here, the observed mean consump- tion is only 11,200 kWh/year, a ratio of just 0.54."
205,This characterizes a severe performance gap where house- holds are consuming 46% less energy than the mod- elled thermodynamic baseline for archetype-standard operating conditions.
207,The 66% difference between the physical requirement and empirical consumption in Oldham (compared to the 265% over-heating in Hampstead) quantifies the mag- nitude of the behavioral decoupling achieved by the framework.
208,"5.6 Spatial Distribution of Rationing Elas- ticity (β inc) Beyond mapping structural efficiency, our model en- ables an analysis of how rationing elasticity varies geographically."
209,"While the global coefficient (β inc= −0.028) confirms the trend of deprivation-driven under-heating, consistent with the spatial clustering visible in Figure 2 and the interactions identified in Figure 4, localized effects suggest acute rationing in peri-urban areas on the fringes of city-regions."
211,"5.7 Computational Stability and Internal Validation To ensure that the recovered behaviorally-adjusted es- timates are grounded in physical reality rather than model artifacts, we conducted internal consistency checks and sensitivity analyses."
212,"Prior Sensitivity Check:Given the use of an informative prior on the global physics bias (β th∼ Normal(−0.3,0.1)), we tested the model’s sensitivity by substituting it with a weakly informative alterna- tive (Normal(0,0.5))."
214,"To ensure method- ological transparency and rapid iteration, this sensi- tivity analysis was conducted on the Greater Manch- ester spatial subset (353 MSOAs), utilizing the identi- cal BYM2 architecture employed in the national run."
217,The significant shift inβ th from the prior mean of -0.3 to the posterior mean of 10 -0.613 (visualized in Figure 6) is justified by the data- driven predictive calibration; prior predictive checks confirmed that the SAP-based theoretical baseline sys- tematically over-predicts consumption.
218,"Specifically, the prior predictive mean residual stood at-0.2903, indicating severe miscalibration."
219,"Following Bayesian updating, the posterior mean residual improved dra- matically to0.0227, confirming this empirical correc- tion successfully aligns the structural estimates with observed metered data."
220,"5.8 Health and Economic Convergent Va- lidity To establish the external relevance of the behaviorally- adjusted metric, we performed a Local Authority (LA) level correlation analysis between the meanT∗and ONS Excess Winter Deaths (EWD) data, alongside a regional correlation against independent energy expen- diture data from the actual UK Household Longitudi- nal Study (UKHLS) Wave 13."
221,"The analysis identifies a statistically significant positive correlation between structural thermal deficiency and winter mortality vul- nerability (r= 0.16, p <0.05 at LAD scale), consis- tent with the determinants of health identified by [28]."
222,"While the effect size is modest (r2= 0.026), reflecting the multi-causal complexity of winter mortality at the district scale, the positive direction is consistent with the expected structural-thermal pathway and warrants further investigation with linked household-level data."
223,"Furthermore, the regional analysis yielded an appar- ently large negative correlation (r=−0.6667) between T∗and UKHLS mean annual gas expenditure."
224,"While this result is marginally non-significant (p= 0.071) due to the small number of regional aggregate degrees of freedom, the high effect size provides suggestive pre- liminary evidence for the behavioral rationing char- acterized by the model, though the small number of regional aggregate observations (n= 9) limits the in- terpretability of this finding: regions with the highest structural inefficiency exhibit significantly lower abso- lute metered spend, confirming that physical deficiency leads to acute energy rationing rather than increased expenditure in the absence of affordability."
225,"6 Limitations While the framework achieves massive spatial scalabil- ity, we explicitly acknowledge several theoretical limi- tations stemming from partial residualization."
229,"(2018) [29], the mean-field approx-imation converges to a saddle point of the Evidence Lower Bound that systematically underestimates pos- terior variance and may bias the posterior means of highly correlated spatial random effects."
